          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. The surrounding qualification limits its extent but does not weaken the clear avowal form.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"The expression invokes Cordelia's existing filial bond and the duties owed by a daughter, rather than making a distinct new promise. Cordelia identifies herself as bound to return appropriate love, obedience, and honour to Lear; no separate undertaking by Lear or explicit uptake occurs.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal words are a controlled, carefully delimited answer to Lear's demand, not a reflex-like affective outburst. Cordelia explicitly frames her difficulty as putting her heart into speech and measures the avowal as “no more nor less.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.87,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and invoking filial obligation, the expression functions as a restrained refusal to participate in Lear's competitive, hyperbolic declaration of love. Its limiting language presents plain truthfulness and rejects exaggeration.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"later_1\",\"quotation\":\"So young, my lord, and true.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most while distributing their dowers. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives this measured declaration, addressing Lear both as father and sovereign and limiting her claim to the love appropriate to her filial bond. Lear rejects the answer and urges her to improve it.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing daughter-to-father bond and its duties: appropriate love, obedience, and honour. She does not clearly create a new promise in the focal words. Lear does not accept her formulation; he treats it as inadequate and subsequently disowns her. Later supplied passages support her continuing affection and care, but these do not convert the original avowal into a separate promise.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The source explicitly identifies William Shakespeare's The Tragedy of King Lear, and I recognise the scene as Lear's love test involving Cordelia.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied text strongly supports the avowal's linguistic force and Cordelia's affection, but the exact legal or moral scope of her “bond” is not formally specified. The classification distinguishes the focal avowal from Cordelia's neighbouring statements of duty and from her later conduct.\"}"
        }
      ],
